Task 1: Using man and info for Documentation
Subtask 1.1: Using man Pages
Open a terminal and type the following command to view the manual page for the ls command:

man ls
Use arrow keys to navigate
Press /search_term to search (e.g., /recursive)
Press q to quit
Search for a specific section in the man page:

man 5 passwd
This shows documentation for the /etc/passwd file format (section 5)
List all available sections for a command:

man -f passwd
Expected Outcome: You should see detailed documentation for the ls command and the /etc/passwd file structure.

Subtask 1.2: Using info Pages
View the info documentation for coreutils (includes basic commands like ls, cp):

info coreutils
Navigate with arrow keys
Press Enter on hyperlinked items
Press q to quit
Search for a specific topic:

info coreutils ls invocation
Expected Outcome: You should see structured documentation with hyperlinks for GNU core utilities.

Task 2: Navigating and Searching Documentation
Subtask 2.1: Searching Man Pages
Search for man pages containing "network":

man -k network
or (equivalent):

apropos network
Update the man page database (if results are outdated):

sudo mandb
Expected Outcome: A list of man pages related to "network".

Subtask 2.2: Using --help and -h Flags
Get quick help for grep:
grep --help
or:
grep -h
Expected Outcome: A concise help message showing command options.

Task 3: Accessing Package-Specific Documentation
Subtask 3.1: Exploring /usr/share/doc
List documentation for installed packages:

ls /usr/share/doc
View documentation for a specific package (e.g., bash):

ls /usr/share/doc/bash
Read a README file (example for bash):

less /usr/share/doc/bash/README
Expected Outcome: You should see package-specific documentation files (READMEs, changelogs, licenses).

Subtask 3.2: Installing Documentation for a Package
Install the httpd (Apache) documentation:

sudo dnf install httpd-manual  # RHEL/Fedora/CentOS
or:

sudo apt install apache2-doc   # Debian/Ubuntu
Access the documentation:

ls /usr/share/doc/httpd-manual
Expected Outcome: Additional documentation files for the httpd package.

Troubleshooting Tips
If man or info commands don't work, install them:
sudo dnf install man-db info  # RHEL/Fedora/CentOS
sudo apt install man-db info  # Debian/Ubuntu
If /usr/share/doc is empty, some packages may not install docs by default. Use dnf or apt to install -doc or -doc sub-packages.
